.TH CONN 1 "" "conn 0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
conn \- a station for one operator directing many hands on one machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B conn
.br
.B conn down
.br
.B conn theme claude
.br
.B conn theme vim
.br
.B conn \-\-light
.br
.B conn \-\-dark
.br
.B conn \-\-help
.br
.B conn \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
conn shows the hands working each place, what each is doing, and which is
waiting on the operator, and puts the operator in front of one.
.SH GENERAL
.TP
1-1.
\fBPURPOSE.\fR This manual describes conn and its operation.
.TP
1-2.
\fBSCOPE.\fR conn is a station for one operator directing many hands on one
machine. It shows the hands working each place, the standing of each, and which
hand is waiting on the operator, and it puts the operator in front of a hand. It
does not edit files, run builds, or hold conversations. The hands do.
.TP
1-3.
\fBDEFINITIONS.\fR Terms used in this manual.
.RS
.TP
\fBOperator\fR
The person at the station.
.TP
\fBHand\fR
A process working a place for the operator: a shell, an agent, or a run. Also
station hand.
.TP
\fBAgent\fR
A hand that holds a conversation and can stop to wait on the operator.
.TP
\fBPlace\fR
A directory hands work in: a git repository, or a folder holding several.
.TP
\fBStanding\fR
What a hand is doing: WORKING, ACTIVE, IDLE, or WAITING.
.TP
\fBWaiting\fR
The standing of an agent stopped on something it has asked of the operator.
.TP
\fBRail\fR
The pane on the left, forty-four columns wide, holding the watch and the other
views.
.TP
\fBWatch\fR
The hands, by place, in the order they began.
.TP
\fBSlot\fR
The pane on the right, holding one hand.
.TP
\fBBar\fR
The line at the foot of the window: an annunciator for the keys, and a lamp for
each hand.
.RE
.TP
1-4.
\fBINDICATIONS.\fR The station indicates two things: what each hand is doing,
and which hand is waiting. The watch and the bar's lamps indicate the first.
WAITING on the watch, blinking, a lamp in the owed color, and \fBtab\fR indicate
the second.
.TP
1-5.
\fBCOMMANDS.\fR \fBconn\fR alone brings up the console and continues to the
watch. Other commands are given by name after it. \fB\-\-light\fR and
\fB\-\-dark\fR set the ground, ahead of a command if one is given.
.PP
.I "SYNOPSIS"
.RS
.TP
\fBconn\fR
Brings up the console, then the watch.
.TP
\fBconn down\fR
Takes the server down with everything in it, and reports what went.
.TP
\fBconn theme claude\fR
Writes conn's theme for Claude Code.
.TP
\fBconn theme vim\fR
Writes conn's colorscheme for nvim.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-light\fR
Sets the ground light.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-dark\fR
Sets the ground dark.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-help\fR
Prints this table.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-version\fR
Prints the build.
.RE
